          <h2>The short answer</h2>
          <p>Yes, often. A clinic that offers direct billing submits your claim for you at the appointment and collects the insurer share itself. Three things have to line up. The clinic must be set up with a billing network or an insurer portal. Your insurer must take part in that route. And your plan must allow the payment to go to the clinic rather than to you. When any one of those pieces is missing, the visit follows the older route: you pay the clinic, keep the receipt, submit the claim yourself and wait for the insurer to pay you back. Both routes draw on the same physiotherapy coverage. Direct billing changes who sends the claim and who waits for the money, never what your plan covers.</p>

          <h2>What happens at the appointment</h2>
          <p>You give the clinic your plan and member numbers, often when you book or at your first visit. After your treatment, the clinic sends the claim electronically with the service, the date and the fee. Many submissions come back with an answer at once, so the clinic can tell you before you leave what the insurer paid and what share remains. You pay the remaining share at the desk, and the clinic gives you a receipt or claim summary showing the fee, the amount the insurer covered and the amount you paid. Keep that record. If you later claim a leftover amount under a second plan or a spending account, that summary is the paper you will need.</p>

          <h2>The networks behind the counter</h2>
          <p>Most direct billing runs through a small number of networks and portals. TELUS Health runs eClaims, a service that lets clinics submit claims to many insurers at once. TELUS describes eClaims as a way for providers to submit claims on behalf of their patients at the appointment, and the service is free for providers and patients. Clinics register with TELUS, then send claims from a portal, an app or their booking software. The TELUS list of participating insurers includes Canada Life and Manulife, along with Desjardins, Equitable, Beneva, the Chambers of Commerce Group Insurance Plan and many plan administrators, and physiotherapists appear on the TELUS list of eligible providers.</p>
          <p>Two large insurers run their own doors. Sun Life is not on the TELUS list. Clinics that bill Sun Life register for a Sun Life Connect account and submit through Sun Life eClaims, and Sun Life lists physiotherapists among the specialties the service accepts. Green Shield providers use providerConnect, the Green Shield portal, where a provider can check member eligibility, submit a claim for adjudication right away and choose whether the payment goes to the clinic or to the member. Clinics often register with more than one route, which is why two clinics in the same city can give you different answers about the same insurer.</p>

          <h2>When direct billing is not an option</h2>
          <p>Sometimes the answer at the desk is no, and the reason is usually administrative rather than medical. The clinic may not be registered with any network. Your plan may pay members only and refuse payment assignment to a clinic, a rule Sun Life describes on its provider pages. Or you may hold coverage under two plans, where coordination of benefits rules decide which plan pays first and which pays second, and the second claim can need its own separate submission. In each case the pay and claim route still works, as long as your plan covers the visit itself.</p>

          <h2>What to confirm before you book</h2>
          <p>Direct billing settles the claim. It does not settle your coverage. Before your first visit, confirm the points that decide what you will actually pay:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Your plan includes physiotherapy, and how much of your annual maximum you have left</li>
            <li>The share of each visit your plan pays, and any limit it sets on the amount paid for one visit</li>
            <li>Whether your plan asks for a referral from a physician before it pays physiotherapy claims, since some insurers still require one even though you can book a physiotherapist directly</li>
            <li>Whether the clinic bills your insurer directly, and which network or portal it uses to do it</li>
            <li>What the first visit costs, and what you would owe if the claim comes back lower than you expected</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The same checks apply across the physiotherapy family. Pelvic floor physiotherapy, vestibular rehabilitation and shockwave therapy all claim under physiotherapy on most plans, so the questions you ask for a regular visit are the questions to ask for those visits too.</p>

          <h2>Questions patients ask</h2>
          <h3>Does direct billing mean my visit costs me nothing?</h3>
          <p>No. Direct billing settles the insurer share at the desk. You still pay the part your plan leaves behind, such as the percentage it does not cover and anything you book after your annual maximum runs out.</p>
          <h3>Can the clinic tell me exactly what my plan covers before I book?</h3>
          <p>Only partly. Networks answer when a claim or an estimate goes in, and some insurers accept an estimate before treatment. Clinics generally cannot see your full plan, so your plan booklet and your insurer app remain the reliable source for your maximums and your referral rules.</p>
          <h3>My plan is with Sun Life. Why did the clinic say it cannot direct bill?</h3>
          <p>The clinic may not be registered with Sun Life Connect, or your plan may not allow payment to go to the clinic. If the clinic is registered and your plan allows it, Sun Life claims go through the Sun Life portal rather than through TELUS eClaims.</p>
          <h3>What if the claim is refused at the desk?</h3>
          <p>You pay the clinic, take the receipt and submit the claim yourself. A refusal at the desk often comes from a setup detail or a plan rule rather than from the treatment. Ask the clinic what response it received, then check that point with your insurer before your next visit.</p>

          <h2>Find physiotherapy care that fits how you pay</h2>
          <p>Start with the treatment, then ask about billing. Browse the <a href="/#directory">WellFinder directory</a> to compare clinics across Canada, and open pages such as <a href="/clinics-pelvic-floor-physiotherapy">pelvic floor physiotherapy clinics</a> and <a href="/clinics-vestibular-rehabilitation">vestibular rehabilitation clinics</a> to see providers in the physiotherapy family. When you call to book, ask whether the clinic will bill your insurer directly.</p>
          <p>WellFinder is an independent directory and is not affiliated with TELUS Health, Sun Life, Green Shield or any other insurer. This guide explains direct billing practice in Canada. Your plan governs your coverage, and the clinic and your insurer can confirm the details.</p>

